# Lore drafts (canon: soft): places in and beyond the academy.

@lore the_village
keys: village, barge, market square, market day, tailor, pawnbroker
canon: soft
text: The village lies across the lake, reached by barge. On its square stand the bakery's trestle, the tailor's rail of robes and the pawnbroker's creaking sign; on market days Mir keeps a stall there. Round the square are the Gilded Newt, Mabel's tea shop, Grimsby's Books and Pell & Daughters, and their shopkeepers have seen every kind of student.

@lore gilded_newt
keys: Gilded Newt, tavern, landlady
canon: soft
text: The village tavern, with low beams and a fire that never goes out. By day it's a few regulars and the landlady with a cloth over her shoulder; by night it's all elbows and cider. Students work shifts there and card games run late.

@lore mabels
keys: Mabel's, tea shop
canon: soft
text: Forty kinds of tea and three kinds of cake. Mabel reads your mood from the way you open the door.

@lore grimsbys
keys: Grimsby's Books, bookshop
canon: soft
text: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean on each other. Mr Grimsby will find anything except what you came in for.

@lore pell_and_daughters
keys: Pell & Daughters, apothecary, Old Pell
canon: soft
text: The village apothecary, with a thousand labelled drawers. Old Pell taught three generations to count drops; his daughters keep the counter now, and he counts aloud behind them. It buys what foragers bring out of the Whisperwood.

@lore bakery
keys: bakery, baker's daughter
canon: soft
text: The same family has baked in the village for two hundred years. A great-great-great-grandmother waited all her life for a letter from a student who died.

@lore whisperwood
keys: Whisperwood, forest, moonpetal, shadow stag, heartwood, waystone
canon: soft
text: An old forest west of the lake that talks in its sleep. The path through it is safe; off it, the wood goes down and down: the fringe, the thicket, the red-barked heartwood, and at the bottom the Hush. Foragers leave offerings at a worn waystone on the way in, and Pell buys what they bring out. A stag made of shadow walks the deep wood and steps aside only for the brave; Professor Brask bows to it in the bracken.

@lore secret_garden
keys: secret garden, silver bees, humming pear tree
canon: soft
text: Behind the potting shed, a door in the wall leads to Maud's secret garden: glowing ferns, silver bees and a pear tree that hums. It only exists for people Maud has shown it to.

@lore greenhouses
keys: greenhouses, moonflower
canon: soft
text: Glass houses that steam even in winter. The moonflowers there bloom once every eleven years and fill the greenhouse with silver light.

@lore observatory
keys: observatory, telescope, east tower
canon: soft
text: A brass dome atop the east tower. The great telescope points wherever it likes. On cold nights Kael sits on the roof outside it, and frost spreads around him.

@lore boathouse
keys: boathouse, jetty
canon: soft
text: An old boathouse with a jetty over the water, where people say the things they can't say indoors. The tunnel to the round door starts here.

@lore thornwick
keys: Thornwick Academy, rival school
canon: soft
text: The rival academy across the hills, famous for illusionists and tournament grudges. Exchange students come and go with suspicious politeness.

@lore the_north
keys: the North, Northern, ice palaces, singing glaciers, northern lights
canon: soft
text: The frozen North of ice palaces, singing glaciers and green-and-violet lights in the sky. Northerners touch foreheads in farewell and practise a frost-craft older than anything taught at Lumenhold.

@lore port_calder
keys: Port Calder, coast, street origin
canon: soft
text: A rough harbour town on the coast, where students of the street origin learned their trade and where trunks get stolen only once per visit.

@lore conclave
keys: Conclave, Conclave of the Seven Towers
canon: soft
text: The governing body of magic, in the capital. It asks professors for recommendations and runs laboratories where new spells are tested behind thick walls.

@lore capital_institutions
keys: Royal Library, Guild of Alchemists, Office of Unseen Affairs, Travelling Circus of Wonders
canon: soft
text: Beyond the academy lie the Royal Library's archives, the Guild of Alchemists' works, the Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs, which has no sign and no address, and the Travelling Circus of Wonders, which recruits graduates who want to wander.

# World expansion 1: the Whisperwood delve (content/events/whisperwood/)

@lore the_hush
keys: the Hush, Hush
canon: soft
text: The deepest part of the Whisperwood, past the heartwood, which wakes only at midnight. Its moonpetal blooms by the clearing-full, and its silence is a thing you can walk into.

@lore the_spinner
keys: the Spinner, Tuppence, Spinner, Spinner silk
canon: soft
text: A spider as big as a carriage, grey, with a voice like a cello, who rules the Hush and keeps worse things out of it. Old Maud found her as a hand-sized hatchling in the greenhouses in her first winter as groundskeeper, called her Tuppence, and let her go into the wood when she outgrew the potting shed. She has a blind left eye and receives guests on her left side, as is proper. Her oldest web holds sixty years of things foragers dropped, and her grey silk drinks the light.

@lore absalom_vrell
keys: Absalom Vrell, the Outcast, Vrell
canon: soft
text: A wizard expelled from Lumenhold for forbidden practice before the current students' parents were born; his name is still in the school ledger, crossed out. He waits in the Hush, by a lantern, with a flask of good tea, for the Spinner to fall. He taught the thornwitch and the masked dabblers in the wood. A grandmaster of every forbidden spell, courteous and bored; nobody has challenged him in forty years. He is not part of any plot but his own.

@lore thornwitch
keys: thornwitch, thorn wand
canon: soft
text: A hedge-practitioner in a cloak sewn with thorns who drinks the life of trees in the Whisperwood's thicket with Siphon, and offers apprentices a blackthorn wand.

@lore whisperwood_creatures
keys: bramble ferret, bristleback, stag beetle, wisps, pixies, ember foxes, grumpy oak, spiderlings, talking trees
canon: soft
text: The Whisperwood's lesser folk: bristly bramble ferrets that rob foragers' bags, bristlebacks (quilled bears, slow and easily offended), stag beetles the size of ponies, pixies who keep a toll stone, riddling wisps and hollow oaks, oaks that take a stubbed toe personally, coin-sized spiderlings by the hundred in the deep wood, and foxes with ember tails who will walk a polite student through. The old trees gossip about the students who walk under them.

@lore elder_oak
keys: elder oak, pale ring, the cold spring
canon: soft
text: The oldest tree in the Whisperwood, in the heartwood, with the other trees grown round it like courtiers and a face in its bark with its eyes shut. Asked politely, it shows its rings: sixty in from the bark, one is pale as frost, for "the cold spring". It gives a splinter of heartwood to someone it judges worthy.

@lore masked_circle
keys: masked circle, white masks, chalk circle, Compel
canon: soft
text: Three hedge-dabblers in plain white masks who practise Compel in a chalk circle in the heartwood. Their chalk marks are copied, badly, from somewhere; they don't know what the marks are for. Absalom Vrell taught them, after a fashion.

@lore wood_witch
keys: wood's witch, ghost on the log, last witch
canon: soft
text: A ghost who knits nothing on a fallen log in the Hush. She was the wood's witch before the castle, before the lake was a lake, and left when the Spinner came; she still can't decide if that was cowardice or manners. She knows the Spinner's weakness.

@lore village_tailor
keys: village tailor, ward thread, ward-stitched cloak
canon: soft
text: The tailor on the village square, who exchanges students' ruined robes at half price and, it's said, feels it whenever a sleeve is singed. She sews Sister Amaranth's ward thread into cloaks, and will make a robe of Spinner silk, at a price "for my nerves".

@lore pawnbroker
keys: pawnbroker, stuffed owl, loupe
canon: soft
text: The village pawnbroker, under a stuffed owl, turns each thing twice through his loupe as if it hid a second, cheaper thing underneath. He buys back what students are tired of.

@lore night_market
keys: night market, Night Market, far field, hooded sellers
canon: soft
text: On the moonless night of every term month, a market appears after midnight in a field past the village's last stile, and by dawn it's grass again. Its sellers are hooded and name no price until asked; some take payment that isn't coin. In summer it has been seen on other nights, in other fields. The village pretends not to know about it, and most of the village has been once.

# The capital and the countryside, round 2 (new maps, 2026-11-11; docs/maps-plan.md)

@lore crew_loft
keys: the crew, the crew's loft, Nan Merrow, Jory, the loft
canon: soft
text: The street-raised player's crew lives in a loft above a chandler's in Port Calder: hammocks, a smoking stove and a card game that has never stopped. Nan Merrow runs the loft and most of the street, deals everyone in, and would rather one of her own got out clean. Jory, one of the crew, is serving a year in the city gaol after getting caught at the bonded warehouses.

@lore undercroft
keys: the Undercroft, Undercroft
canon: soft
text: The old cellars under the capital's middle bridge, flooded once a century and dry enough in between: a gambling hall of dice and cards, a sawdust pit for prize-fights and the fences who buy what the docks lose. The watch knows it is there and is paid not to.

@lore rookery
keys: the Rookery, Rookery
canon: soft
text: The rooftops of Port Calder, where the old harbour town's houses lean close enough to cross a street without touching it. The street crews have run its routes for generations and chalk their marks on its chimneys.

@lore bonded_warehouses
keys: the bonded warehouses, bonded warehouses, the customs' strongroom, the foreman
canon: soft
text: The customs' warehouses on Port Calder's wharf, where cargo waits under the crown's red wax seal until its duty is paid: a glass-topped wall, yard dogs, a watchman's round every hour and an iron strongroom of seized goods. Their foreman has for years kept two sets of figures and sold the difference by a side door; the night Jory of the crew was caught on the floor, he was sent down for a crate the foreman had already sold.

@lore crescent_house
keys: the house on the Crescent, the shipowner, the shipowner's safe
canon: soft
text: The tallest house on the Crescent belongs to a shipowner who bought his way up the hill from Port Calder's wharves. Every week he's in town he gives a ball, a garden ball in summer, to prove he belongs there; the old Crescent families come for his supper table and say what they like on the way home. Much of his fortune came up the hill by the bonded warehouses' side door, from the foreman's second set of figures.

@lore tide_caves
keys: the Tide Caves, tide caves, the sea's back door, the sea's door
canon: soft
text: Under the headland past Port Calder's breakwater, sea caves open at low water and close on the flood. Smugglers have run goods through their dry upper galleries for generations, under the customs men's feet; a brick culvert climbs from them to the bonded warehouses. At the far end a great arch, the sea's door, opens on the open sea, with a ship carved over it by nobody knows whom.

@lore capital_districts
keys: the capital, uptown, the Crescent, midtown, the works, Port Calder, Lamplighters, lamplighters' guild
canon: soft
text: The capital grew down its river: uptown, where the Crescent's great houses curve along the hill above the Royal Gardens; midtown, with the Grand Bazaar, the Arcade of Services, the theatre and the three old bridges the lamplighters' guild lights at every dusk; the works quarter, all chimneys and clockworks; and Port Calder at the river's mouth, the old harbour town the city swallowed, with its fish market, its docks and its crooked lanes.

@lore cathedral_of_the_lamp
keys: Cathedral of the Lamp, the Lamp, the cathedral
canon: soft
text: The capital's great church, whose spire is seen from the sea. Its lamp has burned for four hundred years; its bells tell the whole city the hour; its crypt keeps archives older than the Royal Library's.

@lore countryside_country
keys: the countryside, the valley, the upland, the high road, the tarn, the ford, Hollin Coppice, the Drovers' Inn
canon: soft
text: The countryside is a long valley with two villages on one road: fen in the south-west, moor in the east, the ridge and its standing stones in the north. Up on the upland side lie the tarn and the farms outside the villages; the high road runs west past the old quarry to the Drovers' Inn; the road crosses the river at the ford; Hollin Coppice is the old cut wood in the south.

# New maps, round 2 (K4): the valley's secret, "The Good Year".
@lore good_year
keys: the Good Year, the valley's bargain, the seventh year, the Guest of the Year, stays on
canon: soft
text: An old bargain, older than the villages' names: every seventh year the valley lends a stranger, usually a traveller lodging at the coaching inn, to the Tenant under the hollow hill on the moor, and the harvests stay good. The keepers tell themselves the lent come home seven years on; they don't. They sit at the Tenant's supper, unaged and remembering nothing of home, for as long as the bargain holds; the coats they came in wear the scarecrows. Break the bargain and they all walk out, and the luck goes with them. The keepers are the coaching inn's landlord, who chooses the guest and keeps the guest book ("stays on"), and the miller, who counts the dry wheel's turns to the supper; the almanac prints a harvest page for "the Guest of the Year" that the keepers cut out. The Marsh's hedge-witch keeps who she can out of it with rue. The upland farms, outside the villages' land, have no share in the luck.

@lore the_tenant
keys: the Tenant, the Tenant's supper, the Tenant's table, the hall under the hill
canon: soft
text: The Tenant keeps a long hall under the hollow hill on the moor, where a harvest supper is always laid. It is tall and wheat-crowned, with a face like a field seen from a moving cart, courteous and hungry for company rather than flesh: its guests eat, laugh and do not age at its table for as long as the bargain holds. It answers the door in the hill only to someone who knows what is behind it.

@lore old_hall
keys: the old hall, the valley's old family
canon: soft
text: The valley's shut manor behind its wall at the moor's edge. The family who built it kept the valley's books, the ones that matter, until they sold up and left in a bad year nobody will date; an old caretaker stayed. This year a land agent from the capital bought it and works at the old family's desk.

@lore land_agent
keys: the land agent, the man in good boots, the land company, the agent's principals
canon: soft
text: A land agent from the capital, in good city boots, buying the valley's upland farms cheap this year for a company with an office uptown. He has bought the old hall and read its ledgers, and means to improve the arrangement: a guest for the Tenant every year, from the company's own hand, and the luck to follow the company's land.
